description:
Path: crate-jmap-server/benches/dispatcher.rs:134-143. The bench body does b.iter(|| { let req = parse_request(black_box(body.clone()), 16).expect(...); ... }). The body.clone() runs INSIDE the iter closure, so its cost is counted in the timing for parse_request_three_calls. For a 3-call sample with ~10 JSON keys, clone() walks the Value tree and allocates ~12 Map/Vec/String heap blocks — that's likely larger than the work parse_request itself does (which is mostly a single serde_json::from_value walk + a length check).

Today's bench measures parse_request + Value::clone rather than parse_request alone. A future optimization to parse_request that halves its real cost would not show as a 2x speedup in this bench; it would show as a much smaller speedup because the clone dominates.

The comment at line 136-140 acknowledges this ('The clone cost is unavoidable but stable across runs, so the bench remains a fair regression baseline'). The 'stable across runs' part is true; the 'fair regression baseline' part is suspect — a regression in parse_request itself will be masked by the constant-cost clone.

Fix: use Criterion's iter_batched with BatchSize::SmallInput, building a fresh Value once per batch but cloning outside the measured region. Pattern:
  c.bench_function("parse_request_three_calls", |b| {
      b.iter_batched(
          || body.clone(),
          |body| parse_request(black_box(body), 16).expect(...),
          criterion::BatchSize::SmallInput,
      )
  });
Same applies to bench_resolve_args_with_ref and bench_resolve_args_no_ref (lines 146-167), which also clone inside iter.

Expected impact at scale: 2-5× cleaner signal in regression catch — the bench will surface a real parse_request regression instead of averaging it against a constant clone cost. Workspace tracking: pairs with bd:JMAP-sc1b.18 which set up the bench infrastructure.
